                <pb facs="0755" n="621" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg621"/>

                <head>No. 451.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Pendleton to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-03-17"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 17, 1888</date>.
                        (Received April 3.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 593.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose herewith the
                    originals and translations of a proclamation to the people of Germany, and an
                    open letter to Prince Bismarck, as the head of the ministry, both made public
                    last Monday by the Emperor Frederick III, immediately after his arrival at
                    Charlottenburg last Sunday evening. It seems to me that there is a noble
                    frankness about both documents, delineating a policy to be pursued by the
                    acceding Emperor, full of promise for the German people, and extremely
                    creditable to him.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Geo. H.
                    Pendleton.</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d451-a1.xml" xpointer="d451-a1"/>
